'''Lycan, S.A.''' is a Balboan {{w|polyester}} manufacturer based in [[Badia Curta]], [[Jala Province|Jala]]. The company produces {{w|polyester chip}}s and {{w|polyester|filaments}} for use in {{w|textile manufacturing}}, {{w|polyethylene terephthalate|PET}} packaging, and industrial applications. It is listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] under the ticker LYC and is a constituent of the [[SCX 100]] index.

Lycan operates at the upstream end of the synthetic fiber supply chain, producing polyester chips from {{w|purified terephthalic acid}} and {{w|ethylene glycol}} sourced from the [[Badia Curta]] chemical complex. The company's chips are sold to downstream fiber manufacturers, including [[Opal (Company)|Opal]], as well as to packaging and film producers. Lycan also produces polyester filaments and staple fiber directly for the textile industry.

== History ==

Lycan was founded in 1979 by Bernat Lladó i Segarra, a chemical engineer who had previously managed {{w|polymerisation}} operations at [[Companyia Química Occidental]]. Lladó recognised that the Badia Curta cluster produced sufficient {{w|purified terephthalic acid|PTA}} and {{w|ethylene glycol|MEG}} feedstocks to support a dedicated polyester chip plant, and that Balboa's textile industry was importing chips from overseas suppliers at significant cost.

The company built its first polymerisation line at Badia Curta in 1981, with initial output of approximately 30,000 tonnes per year of polyester chips. The facility's location within the chemical complex provided direct pipeline access to PTA and MEG from adjacent producers, reducing feedstock transportation costs.

In the late 1980s, Lycan expanded into filament production, adding spinning lines to produce {{w|polyester|partially oriented yarn}} and {{w|polyester|drawn textured yarn}} for textile manufacturers. The expansion positioned Lycan as both an intermediate supplier to other fiber producers and a direct supplier to the textile industry.

The company was listed on the [[Bolsa de Sant Cristòfor]] in 1992. The [[Fons d'Inversió Directa de Balboa]] acquired a strategic stake in 2001 as part of its investment in the domestic petrochemical value chain.

Through the 2000s and 2010s, Lycan expanded its polymerisation capacity and modernised its spinning lines, increasing annual output to approximately 120,000 tonnes of chips and 45,000 tonnes of filaments and staple fiber.

== Operations ==

Lycan operates a single production complex at [[Badia Curta]], with direct pipeline connections to feedstock suppliers within the chemical cluster. The complex comprises:

* Polymerisation lines producing {{w|polyethylene terephthalate|PET}} chips for fiber, film, and packaging applications
* Spinning lines producing {{w|polyester|partially oriented yarn}} and {{w|polyester|drawn textured yarn}}
* Staple fiber production lines

Approximately 50 per cent of Lycan's chip output is sold to downstream manufacturers, including [[Opal (Company)|Opal]], with the remainder processed internally into filaments and staple fiber. The company exports approximately 40 per cent of its finished products, primarily to [[Tapuya]], [[Potocsí]], and [[Balisca]].

